600b194b85fca9e3ffd92a91baa71eea07169f8edb87e600babb427628c00022" "q-126922/selection/823df713211f07e2a064d077a7059163efd6fe88f3993f546fc021e6b2ab9aff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126922" :question/id #uuid "6ae0c8fb-626b-544b-9719-70c11c43a0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126922" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126922/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126922"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-149682/selection/204dc65bfec87b7ca97d1089749a24bcbacaa273b2e244791b95b154af58e44d" :answer/id #uuid "72cf8cb7-93e6-5fb8-b9e8-7201eb6db2fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149682/selection/3d9380d19ca7aa33edd41c2b934920d3a0df8268e963c4367871a9b246657394" :answer/id #uuid "0199b376-aa3e-51f5-bc1c-1cf2f7ae0b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149682/selection/759962e84f965f444a1fb2bd8d3fb5940b983b82cd8e2a4a1b4008e20e86f810" :answer/id #uuid "219cfa02-806a-5ff8-9f58-ee8ea3a18534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149682/selection/200ad74612e6b55645d9452cdaea4ecda8b30081381b584dc6e49658cc69ce13" :answer/id #uuid "ef05a31f-2d86-5647-8f48-657ffa8c2d1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149682/selection/871