^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-149682/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126920/selection/dd214943abae3cf52ea57c9b5c26b78412b192c313e4ad336778229e8ba501d5" :answer/id #uuid "dae2d0c6-552f-5cde-a92d-038dc290c8ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126920/selection/b7c5969079f325ebdfb18ff1b3b8