 Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=5,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126920/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126920"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186105967 :question/answer-fields 17592186102049] {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/e9b783bc341a67d442e842b1bf78f7b481b5df14f7815ef397cc2b2c6015a363" :answer/id #uuid "4db618af-8a69-5e48-94fb-801b12557084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/8676eb4896ce919ae825e0983359d2b21b94db12fad3e88e23d3881171fc1490" :answer/id #uuid "f19f71b8-d8c0-57e5-9444-73d6abe4cd11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" :answer/id #uuid "566591da-6b96-581e-9ec6-c1c418c1e0db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/80d1656514931ef2d32ed181d74893243bbcd0bcd2e4b861cb9f008a7191079b" :answer/id #uuid "e2789748-04c4-50cd-8827-f539105a2edc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/96ea96d1e56fba6c6924bd8b8e96a2cfd9ea3287fbf39c6e4e1f59606c1beec5" :answer/id #uuid "cb20230f-a2f4-5a79-a333-b9f89a3ddfca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dcca08d-368c-56de-8a85-69182c2ff363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/e9b783bc341a67d442e842b1bf78f7b481b5df14f7815ef397cc2b2c6015a363" :answer-field/choices ["q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/e9b783bc341a67d442e842b1bf78f7b481b5df14f7815ef397cc2b2c6015a363" "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/8676eb4896ce919ae825e0983359d2b21b94db12fad3e88e23d3881171fc1490" "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/80d1656514931ef2d32ed181d74893243bbcd0bcd2e4b861cb9f008a7191079b" "q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a/96ea96d1e56fba6c6924bd8b8e96a2cfd9ea3287fbf39c6e4e1f59606c1beec5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126830"] :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126830/selection/ma-v1/693bf9fefcc36f66d9597575f97c4bb40f5a56d4d0f4ede0147e4ae83c53f44a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection/c08f4d635c691578b6ea6f9941b4e4d0d130f125b1bb0981ab9f20d89fb86f30" :answer/id #uuid "6a691c9f-0008-5369-9d38-d87f921d1499" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection/0b81dc6b394605019f82a7c19d03f11b53bb51e6a4e16d3057c1fedb41bfacf5" :answer/id #uuid "b808ca10-9dc1-5240-8ce4-79fc6a7d5530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection/0790c8b2b26bc71d5d47aa8459ed6bd9bfd7cedcc99b6ea961848491f2000b54" :answer/id #uuid "7ed5e618-8149-5ce3-a8f9-82e79fda80d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection/35a36dffcc4fa657b5e7205f3dc92e32b16e4c84cc4c0ea69d53050b7a907642" :answer/id #uuid "e36e32bd-2e3b-5081-8a8a-8195953bea76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" :answer/id #uuid "8859cc49-7d10-5c69-93d9-c1f9966fc9e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "074da1c0-2d18-5c65-9b7b-cd11dc0df404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126917/selection/0790c8b2b26bc71d5d47aa8459ed6bd9bfd7cedcc99b6ea961848491f2000b54" :answer-field/choices ["q-126917/selection/c08f4d635c691578b6ea6f9941b4e4d0d130f125b1bb0981ab9f20d89fb86f30" "q-126917/selection/0b81dc6b394605019f82a7c19d03f11b53bb51e6a4e16d3057c1fedb41bfacf5" "q-126917/selection/0790c8b2b26bc71d5d47aa8459ed6bd9bfd7cedcc99b6ea961848491f2000b54" "q-126917/selection/35a36dffcc4fa657b5e7205f3dc92e32b16e4c84cc4c0ea69d53050b7a907642" "q-126917/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126917" :question/id #uuid "0a627b8d-c420-5787-824a-d90fe7d71973" :question/math-academy-id "q-126917" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=2,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126917/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126917"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection/24ed4db1feed7b75c4db70725f81f27269867c15ebf36f195dd52d6822ba6eb5" :answer/id #uuid "16ac3094-3922-53b5-b668-013aa53c5fba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection/30d8b5f7088639ad23e225c387052c84a6aed17222b971bddf29cbc1ade92045" :answer/id #uuid "87e7395f-e4c7-5a13-ac5b-a6d16c5396fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection/69e4591009a94380c5a4fd520c2318f562c77e52d575675825abdfe27211720d" :answer/id #uuid "944cd1d5-0d61-5a86-a25d-4f99cc96a6a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection/c51beed827f06c08863cc7d8e75e3ce77a34b938758ba77f14516402f4129025" :answer/id #uuid "d49ebca8-074c-543e-86a5-9130632eb6e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection/7c16cf9a18a126094fe780c662534f4fba023312b8d976ed0bf275c46048f9a6" :answer/id #uuid "5457778c-9925-5d84-994b-36bec54afb09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c618bf3-5a2d-50d0-86e1-a668c8bc098c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-149688/selection/24ed4db1feed7b75c4db70725f81f27269867c15ebf36f195dd52d6822ba6eb5" :answer-field/choices ["q-149688/selection/24ed4db1feed7b75c4db70725f81f27269867c15ebf36f195dd52d6822ba6eb5" "q-149688/selection/30d8b5f7088639ad23e225c387052c84a6aed17222b971bddf29cbc1ade92045" "q-149688/selection/69e4591009a94380c5a4fd520c2318f562c77e52d575675825abdfe27211720d" "q-149688/selection/c51beed827f06c08863cc7d8e75e3ce77a34b938758ba77f14516402f4129025" "q-149688/selection/7c16cf9a18a126094fe780c662534f4fba023312b8d976ed0bf275c46048f9a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-149688" :question/id #uuid "095fb5b0-13ee-5063-92ab-6c3183cdb095" :question/math-academy-id "q-149688" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-149688/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149688"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126826"] :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection/2e3d839799238ea84ec292051ea8597b1ccce5282ac1eb07901d33654a5d8031" :answer/id #uuid "f31a898b-ccbf-5c80-9b36-cf5887b16ea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection/adea6de6de33b77366fb56ce5e7c5bea8e168241aa8eb80c0701b0fec906ee66" :answer/id #uuid "0592b4d2-c460-5765-9941-58fdaa471987" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection/60125f2a5ca11c4f6d2d10af54070b198671ce30182b1555771acbebcaab5779" :answer/id #uuid "9362173a-7d61-5d05-a272-3f68f2844890" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection/5e9c875c456e1d2157633887ba14e34ac0d95f199fe5486f6384b7a31c8f6c66" :answer/id #uuid "1d0bfe9d-3939-59ae-b9be-6af084c625a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection/5b30f4671c5d62fc7ff7d355bc17b6e023cd6892d3dd3b475396c0539fcbc379" :answer/id #uuid "c0a6ac45-3318-598d-a5ab-fad1ff2863c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12598669-8b2a-54e9-8c78-b3f07c2226e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126927/selection/2e3d839799238ea84ec292051ea8597b1ccce5282ac1eb07901d33654a5d8031" :answer-field/choices ["q-126927/selection/2e3d839799238ea84ec292051ea8597b1ccce5282ac1eb07901d33654a5d8031" "q-126927/selection/adea6de6de33b77366fb56ce5e7c5bea8e168241aa8eb80c0701b0fec906ee66" "q-126927/selection/60125f2a5ca11c4f6d2d10af54070b198671ce30182b1555771acbebcaab5779" "q-126927/selection/5e9c875c456e1d2157633887ba14e34ac0d95f199fe5486f6384b7a31c8f6c66" "q-126927/selection/5b30f4671c5d62fc7ff7d355bc17b6e023cd6892d3dd3b475396c0539fcbc379"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126927" :question/id #uuid "6fd9957a-66b4-5ce3-b176-b797a4c5cba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126927" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126927/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126927"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection/a871c6e0c2f3639b8d6419ebd61a700fc41972e132892ccc8e2164ed9604ed1c" :answer/id #uuid "0bd6c253-3d91-5ba3-872e-af8f561b25a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection/b535f7f49407bfbcbc8e413ce0ed16daf88c0e36857fc168b46f78a469e764e4" :answer/id #uuid "8bef1330-eaae-5e48-8bdd-2e6d57afebfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection/7c16cf9a18a126094fe780c662534f4fba023312b8d976ed0bf275c46048f9a6" :answer/id #uuid "89c0d3e2-b793-5475-92fa-2108163b88aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection/a90e0608c63d5b9534eec358ee6cea830618b2f624a1f4de928e5c59ff4b87b8" :answer/id #uuid "b348b303-6e2b-5ff8-8502-a5d50019517a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection/166d323cc0ce19fedb8e550c7bbbd36225935952a51131a2945ef74b9c857326" :answer/id #uuid "e1cf0c75-c19a-5f0e-a043-feffba6c0927" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0e98dd-673f-585b-90af-48c0fd69e301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126932/selection/166d323cc0ce19fedb8e550c7bbbd36225935952a51131a2945ef74b9c857326" :answer-field/choices ["q-126932/selection/a871c6e0c2f3639b8d6419ebd61a700fc41972e132892ccc8e2164ed9604ed1c" "q-126932/selection/b535f7f49407bfbcbc8e413ce0ed16daf88c0e36857fc168b46f78a469e764e4" "q-126932/selection/7c16cf9a18a126094fe780c662534f4fba023312b8d976ed0bf275c46048f9a6" "q-126932/selection/a90e0608c63d5b9534eec358ee6cea830618b2f624a1f4de928e5c59ff4b87b8" "q-126932/selection/166d323cc0ce19fedb8e550c7bbbd36225935952a51131a2945ef74b9c857326"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126932" :question/id #uuid "d4fb3d46-e8f4-590c-bf7e-7af7a18d54d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126932" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126932/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126932"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" :answer/id #uuid "56ac0d39-6aff-5d6f-955c-8c305dd626a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection/9f74a8f49570085872fc34568b60cf7be31a71230f2e238fdd0cba57e2f4f28c" :answer/id #uuid "332ed1e5-afa8-5f7e-ade8-7bfbd4ef7934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection/9a7e6c0d615e9b744f3e22f1320c84b32692e1f2c745a96cbe114ede9a11cc80" :answer/id #uuid "3376f7da-115f-5182-bedc-b679b49acf31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection/a3aed51e6235b1516e571016ed0d10434e29e76daeb4d7837e8901f1e3d435a3" :answer/id #uuid "32a227c4-b81c-5a78-bb23-721e64c3514f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection/a1dd338b73054fc8651c4fa6fe5f0963983ccbc284d274bb8e03de4d6fbe114c" :answer/id #uuid "9ba30f0f-f3dc-533e-bfe2-e2c5e4978ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf58bc9-51b6-5940-9a44-e4f441652668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-149685/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-149685/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" "q-149685/selection/9f74a8f49570085872fc34568b60cf7be31a71230f2e238fdd0cba57e2f4f28c" "q-149685/selection/9a7e6c0d615e9b744f3e22f1320c84b32692e1f2c745a96cbe114ede9a11cc80" "q-149685/selection/a3aed51e6235b1516e571016ed0d10434e29e76daeb4d7837e8901f1e3d435a3" "q-149685/selection/a1dd338b73054fc8651c4fa6fe5f0963983ccbc284d274bb8e03de4d6fbe114c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-149685" :question/id #uuid "bba80616-72c9-5066-a9a1-cb9a83607d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149685" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-149685/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149685"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9566"] :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0c0a4b177a997ffb38489abc902a1c78d027d0da661b1498fc97d3d9a77862d9.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/0cd188ffe544d454a3375df5f043096888f6dfd5243551d35502efda4bcfeb11.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/5f0d414a5388ae374f46f75825b9ffec7725649a97a45de24f8178d95a94b1ad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9571"] :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/007f19fb0c6e24115d719178a4b44e78c9881b9f71f52d0f2970480358e3faab.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/ec0ddbbff34ed096bb872f23663f97005932a4ea8f368059cc4b511a5c9c76cc.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/assets/6f74bda044d15ed504a0b95b1e60ae8542f2b304f1e2712ae8a1d840a45aeaad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
